WEBVTT

1
00:00:29.625 --> 00:00:31.833
<b>The previous verses discussed</b>

2
00:00:31.833 --> 00:00:34.958
<b>how humans act according </b>
<b>to their inherent nature.</b>

3
00:00:34.958 --> 00:00:37.833
<b>This might lead some to question:</b>

4
00:00:37.833 --> 00:00:41.791
<b>If everyone acts according</b>
<b>to their nature anyway,</b>

5
00:00:41.791 --> 00:00:46.916
<b>what purpose do the scriptural </b>
<b>rules and laws serve?</b>

6
00:00:46.916 --> 00:00:50.208
<b>After all, won't actions simply follow one's nature?</b>

7
00:00:50.208 --> 00:00:53.250
<b>Such arguments often lead people astray.</b>

8
00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:57.125
<b>We must understand this verse in depth.</b>

9
00:01:01.916 --> 00:01:04.791
<b>In this verse, Lord Krishna explains:</b>

10
00:01:04.791 --> 00:01:07.375
<b>Based on feelings of attachment and hatred,</b>

11
00:01:07.375 --> 00:01:14.125
<b>the senses will naturally experience either </b>
<b>attraction or aversion to their objects.</b>

12
00:01:14.125 --> 00:01:20.250
<b>However, one should not fall under the control of </b>
<b>these senses, attachments, and aversions,</b>

13
00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:26.333
<b>as they are obstacles on the path to </b>
<b>self-realization and supreme knowledge.</b>

14
00:01:26.333 --> 00:01:27.666
<b>But the question arises:</b>

15
00:01:27.666 --> 00:01:33.125
<b>If humans are bound to experience attachment </b>
<b>and aversion according to their nature,</b>

16
00:01:33.125 --> 00:01:35.416
<b>what can be done?</b>

17
00:01:35.416 --> 00:01:38.250
<b>The answer lies in regulation and control.</b>

18
00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:40.250
<b>Consider this example...</b>

19
00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:43.708
<b>A child doesn't know right from wrong.</b>

20
00:01:43.708 --> 00:01:48.500
<b>Following their natural hunger instinct, </b>
<b>they might eat sand or dirt.</b>

21
00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:52.000
<b>But when parents guide the child,</b>

22
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:57.125
<b>correcting them when they try to </b>
<b>eat sand or dirt, gradual change occurs.</b>

23
00:01:57.125 --> 00:02:01.333
<b>Initially, the child still feels drawn to the sand/dirt</b>

24
00:02:01.333 --> 00:02:05.375
<b>but restrains themselves due to parental rules.</b>

25
00:02:05.375 --> 00:02:11.541
<b>Gradually, through this regulation, </b>
<b>their attraction to sand/dirt diminishes,</b>

26
00:02:11.541 --> 00:02:15.458
<b>and finally, upon growing up, </b>
<b>they gain the wisdom</b>

27
00:02:15.458 --> 00:02:20.958
<b>to understand how harmful sand</b>
<b>and dirt were for their health.</b>

28
00:02:20.958 --> 00:02:26.875
<b>Here, sand/dirt can be compared </b>
<b>to attachment/aversion.</b>

29
00:02:26.875 --> 00:02:30.166
<b>When a person is spiritually immature,</b>

30
00:02:30.166 --> 00:02:34.833
<b>they're controlled by their natural tendencies </b>
<b>of attachment and aversion.</b>

31
00:02:34.833 --> 00:02:37.208
<b>Though both are harmful,</b>

32
00:02:37.208 --> 00:02:40.958
<b>the person neither knows nor accepts this truth.</b>

33
00:02:40.958 --> 00:02:44.750
<b>Like a child, regulation is necessary here too.</b>

34
00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:49.750
<b>This is where our Vedic knowledge serves</b>
<b>its purpose. The scriptures contain</b>

35
00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:55.041
<b>numerous methods to regulate the senses </b>
<b>and control attachment and aversion,</b>

36
00:02:55.041 --> 00:02:58.125
<b>helping people maintain self-discipline.</b>

37
00:02:58.125 --> 00:03:04.708
<b>Just as a child's attraction to </b>
<b>sand/dirt gradually diminishes,</b>

38
00:03:04.708 --> 00:03:07.458
<b>through following Vedic knowledge,</b>

39
00:03:07.458 --> 00:03:11.208
<b>one begins to gain some control over </b>
<b>their attachments and aversions.</b>

40
00:03:11.208 --> 00:03:17.250
<b>And finally, when one becomes spiritually mature,</b>

41
00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:24.166
<b>they'll understand how harmful these </b>
<b>attachments and aversions were.</b>

42
00:03:24.166 --> 00:03:26.500
<b>But this is a long journey.</b>

43
00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:31.541
<b>It begins with respecting Vedic principles, </b>
<b>followed by practicing them.</b>

44
00:03:31.541 --> 00:03:34.541
<b>Mistakes in this practice are natural,</b>

45
00:03:34.541 --> 00:03:38.583
<b>as each principle must be understood </b>
<b>according to circumstances.</b>

46
00:03:38.583 --> 00:03:39.833
<b>For instance,</b>

47
00:03:39.833 --> 00:03:44.416
<b>while there's a rule to maintain </b>
<b>consistent speed while driving,</b>

48
00:03:44.416 --> 00:03:45.250
<b>however,</b>

49
00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:49.750
<b>the situation differs for a vehicle rushing </b>
<b>a patient to the hospital.</b>

50
00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:53.333
<b>Most of us are spiritually children.</b>

51
00:03:53.333 --> 00:04:00.583
<b>We must study scriptural knowledge, </b>
<b>practice it, reflect on our mistakes,</b>

52
00:04:00.583 --> 00:04:04.666
<b>mature our understanding </b>
<b>according to circumstances...</b>

53
00:04:04.666 --> 00:04:08.000
<b>and gradually, walking this path,</b>

54
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:12.125
<b>we'll progress toward supreme knowledge.</b>

55
00:04:16.458 --> 00:04:19.500
<b>This verse speaks of regulation</b>

56
00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:23.625
<b>and the duality of attraction and aversion.</b>

57
00:04:23.625 --> 00:04:25.375
<b>Consider this:</b>

58
00:04:25.375 --> 00:04:27.625
<b>Imagine being in a situation</b>

59
00:04:27.625 --> 00:04:37.333
<b>where you must choose between </b>
<b>what's right and what's appealing.</b>

60
00:04:37.333 --> 00:04:39.666
<b>For instance,</b>

61
00:04:39.666 --> 00:04:47.541
<b>you're tired and about to </b>
<b>watch an entertaining show,</b>

62
00:04:47.541 --> 00:04:48.791
<b>but</b>

63
00:04:48.791 --> 00:04:56.458
<b>suddenly your child needs help with studies.</b>

64
00:04:56.458 --> 00:05:04.541
<b>One option is attractive, the other is right.</b>

65
00:05:04.541 --> 00:05:12.166
<b>Despite reluctance, helping your </b>
<b>child is the correct choice.</b>

66
00:05:12.166 --> 00:05:13.666
<b>And here,</b>

67
00:05:13.666 --> 00:05:18.541
<b>here you regulated your mind.</b>

68
00:05:18.541 --> 00:05:23.208
<b>The mind naturally gravitates toward </b>
<b>momentary pleasures,</b>

69
00:05:23.208 --> 00:05:29.041
<b>even if they lead to harmful consequences later.</b>

70
00:05:29.041 --> 00:05:33.125
<b>You've likely experienced this –</b>

71
00:05:33.125 --> 00:05:36.791
<b>knowing certain foods aren't healthy,</b>

72
00:05:36.791 --> 00:05:42.375
<b>yet craving them for their taste.</b>

73
00:05:42.375 --> 00:05:43.875
<b>However,</b>

74
00:05:43.875 --> 00:05:48.375
<b>someone conscious of their health</b>

75
00:05:48.375 --> 00:05:58.291
<b>will study the matter and they’ll go against their</b>
<b>taste preferences and reject unhealthy foods,</b>

76
00:05:58.291 --> 00:06:01.166
<b>It begins with one decision,</b>

77
00:06:01.166 --> 00:06:06.458
<b>then gradually, the mind comes under control</b>

78
00:06:06.458 --> 00:06:12.500
<b>and attraction to unhealthy items diminishes.</b>

79
00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:14.000
<b>Similarly,</b>

80
00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:18.750
<b>if you're mindful of spiritual health,</b>

81
00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:22.083
<b>you must study the scriptures.</b>

82
00:06:22.083 --> 00:06:30.333
<b>This reveals which tendencies are</b>
<b>spiritually healthy or unhealthy. </b>

83
00:06:30.333 --> 00:06:39.541
<b>Following scriptural principles helps </b>
<b>you live an ethical and disciplined life.</b>

84
00:06:39.541 --> 00:06:43.208
<b>Remember though –</b>

85
00:06:43.208 --> 00:06:48.416
<b>rules shouldn't be followed blindly,</b>

86
00:06:48.416 --> 00:06:51.958
<b>and the Gita teaches this maturity.</b>

87
00:06:51.958 --> 00:06:55.125
<b>Humans are free to make decisions,</b>

88
00:06:55.125 --> 00:06:58.000
<b>not slaves to rules.</b>

89
00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:05.208
<b>One must understand and </b>
<b>follow rules according to circumstances.</b>

90
00:07:05.208 --> 00:07:07.375
<b>For example,</b>

91
00:07:07.375 --> 00:07:15.583
<b>while it's wrong to interfere </b>
<b>with another's freedom,</b>

92
00:07:15.583 --> 00:07:22.875
<b>what if someone is acting against dharma?</b>

93
00:07:22.875 --> 00:07:28.958
<b>In such cases, upholding dharma</b>
<b>becomes more important.</b>

94
00:07:28.958 --> 00:07:31.666
<b>The Gita teaches that</b>

95
00:07:31.666 --> 00:07:38.166
<b>one must continuously improve </b>
<b>their moral understanding,</b>

96
00:07:38.166 --> 00:07:47.291
<b>making decisions that align with ethics.</b>

97
00:07:47.291 --> 00:07:56.625
<b>Are these decisions solely for personal benefit, </b>
<b>or do they serve society too?</b>

98
00:07:56.625 --> 00:08:00.416
<b>Rules will guide,</b>

99
00:08:00.416 --> 00:08:01.666
<b>but</b>

100
00:08:01.666 --> 00:08:04.666
<b>decisions are yours.</b>

101
00:08:04.666 --> 00:08:06.083
<b>Think about it.</b>

102
00:08:10.833 --> 00:08:14.333
<b>Imagine a typical family</b>

103
00:08:14.333 --> 00:08:20.166
<b>where each member has </b>
<b>their own likes and dislikes –</b>

104
00:08:20.166 --> 00:08:30.000
<b>whether about food, entertainment, </b>
<b>or household purchases.</b>

105
00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:37.583
<b>When your preferences</b>
<b>don't affect others, that's fine.</b>

106
00:08:37.583 --> 00:08:52.250
<b>But when decisions impact the whole family, </b>
<b>family welfare must take priority.</b>

107
00:08:52.250 --> 00:08:54.500
<b>For instance,</b>

108
00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:59.750
<b>if someone wants to play music loudly</b>

109
00:08:59.750 --> 00:09:06.000
<b>while another family member studies for exams,</b>

110
00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:08.000
<b>one must consider</b>

111
00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:17.125
<b>which choice serves momentary </b>
<b>pleasure versus long-term benefits.</b>

112
00:09:17.125 --> 00:09:19.166
<b>The Bhagavad Gita</b>

113
00:09:19.166 --> 00:09:25.625
<b>teaches that public welfare </b>
<b>surpasses personal interest,</b>

114
00:09:25.625 --> 00:09:31.791
<b>and long-term consequences </b>
<b>outweigh momentary pleasures.</b>

115
00:09:31.791 --> 00:09:39.416
<b>Keeping this in mind, sometimes </b>
<b>family rules become necessary –</b>

116
00:09:39.416 --> 00:09:42.375
<b>like dining together</b>

117
00:09:42.375 --> 00:09:47.083
<b>or having a fixed bedtime.</b>

118
00:09:47.083 --> 00:09:49.458
<b>Are these restrictions? </b>

119
00:09:49.458 --> 00:09:50.458
<b>No.</b>

120
00:09:50.458 --> 00:09:58.333
<b>They're guidelines aimed at </b>
<b>everyone's long-term well-being.</b>

121
00:09:58.333 --> 00:10:03.458
<b>Scriptural and Vedic principles should </b>
<b>be viewed in the same light.</b>

122
00:10:03.458 --> 00:10:05.333
<b>They are not restrictions.</b>

123
00:10:05.333 --> 00:10:09.708
<b>These principles weren't created </b>
<b>from one person's whims,</b>

124
00:10:09.708 --> 00:10:10.708
<b>but</b>

125
00:10:10.708 --> 00:10:15.250
<b>were established through centuries </b>
<b>of collective wisdom and experience.</b>

126
00:10:15.250 --> 00:10:19.833
<b>Following them leads to humanity's wellbeing.</b>

127
00:10:19.833 --> 00:10:29.333
<b>The key is choosing according to your </b>
<b>ethics in your specific situation,</b>

128
00:10:29.333 --> 00:10:34.000
<b>that which rule takes precedence.</b>

129
00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:37.250
<b>This applies to family life too...</b>

130
00:10:37.250 --> 00:10:46.416
<b>Instead of commanding, create family rules </b>
<b>through mutual discussion and consensus.</b>

131
00:10:46.416 --> 00:10:51.333
<b>When family members understand </b>
<b>the benefits and drawbacks,</b>

132
00:10:51.333 --> 00:10:56.583
<b>they'll naturally choose right.</b>

133
00:10:56.583 --> 00:11:06.000
<b>This transparency helps everyone, young and </b>
<b>old, feel valued and understand their role.</b>

134
00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:12.083
<b>Rules based on transparency, fairness, </b>
<b>and collective welfare</b>

135
00:11:12.083 --> 00:11:16.791
<b>strengthen family bonds.</b>

136
00:11:16.791 --> 00:11:18.375
<b>Think about it.</b>

137
00:11:22.666 --> 00:11:26.333
<b>Imagine two workplaces:</b>

138
00:11:26.333 --> 00:11:27.666
<b>One</b>

139
00:11:27.666 --> 00:11:34.458
<b>where decisions are based on </b>
<b>personal relationships and immediate gains,</b>

140
00:11:34.458 --> 00:11:44.375
<b>another where decisions consider collective </b>
<b>benefit and long-term organizational goals.</b>

141
00:11:44.375 --> 00:11:49.833
<b>Which is right, and where </b>
<b>would you prefer working?</b>

142
00:11:49.833 --> 00:11:51.500
<b>Certainly,</b>

143
00:11:51.500 --> 00:11:59.458
<b>you'd choose the workplace </b>
<b>that considers collective welfare.</b>

144
00:11:59.458 --> 00:12:04.500
<b>Yet, despite this clarity,</b>

145
00:12:04.500 --> 00:12:15.750
<b>leaders sometimes make decisions based on </b>
<b>personal preferences and individual gain.</b>

146
00:12:15.750 --> 00:12:20.500
<b>They might see immediate benefits,</b>

147
00:12:20.500 --> 00:12:22.833
<b>Yet it's certain</b>

148
00:12:22.833 --> 00:12:29.000
<b>that such decisions not only </b>
<b>cloud their judgment over time,</b>

149
00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:29.958
<b>but</b>

150
00:12:29.958 --> 00:12:33.958
<b>the organization too will have </b>
<b>to bear its consequences.</b>

151
00:12:33.958 --> 00:12:37.541
<b>The Gita teaches that</b>

152
00:12:37.541 --> 00:12:43.125
<b>public welfare supersedes personal preferences,</b>

153
00:12:43.125 --> 00:12:49.791
<b>and long-term results matter </b>
<b>more than immediate gains.</b>

154
00:12:49.791 --> 00:13:02.291
<b>Any leader following these principles will </b>
<b>not only succeed but earn respect as well.</b>

155
00:13:02.291 --> 00:13:04.625
<b>Consider an example:</b>

156
00:13:04.625 --> 00:13:09.916
<b>selecting people for an important project.</b>

157
00:13:09.916 --> 00:13:11.708
<b>What's better?</b>

158
00:13:11.708 --> 00:13:17.583
<b>A senior officer choosing based </b>
<b>on personal preference?</b>

159
00:13:17.583 --> 00:13:21.916
<b>Or adopting a transparent process</b>

160
00:13:21.916 --> 00:13:27.833
<b>where anyone with relevant </b>
<b>experience can apply?</b>

161
00:13:27.833 --> 00:13:33.125
<b>Open nominations not only select the </b>
<b>best and most interested candidates</b>

162
00:13:33.125 --> 00:13:34.291
<b>but</b>

163
00:13:34.291 --> 00:13:40.625
<b>also build organizational </b>
<b>trust through transparency.</b>

164
00:13:40.625 --> 00:13:43.291
<b>Long-term results,</b>

165
00:13:43.291 --> 00:13:45.000
<b>public welfare,</b>

166
00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:47.333
<b>and transparency –</b>

167
00:13:47.333 --> 00:13:49.166
<b>These three principles</b>

168
00:13:49.166 --> 00:13:57.125
<b>are more precious than diamonds and </b>
<b>pearls for anyone in a leadership position.</b>

169
00:13:57.125 --> 00:13:58.291
<b>And</b>

170
00:13:58.291 --> 00:14:04.375
<b>deep wisdom about all these will </b>
<b>come to you through this Gita.</b>

